Normal CSS vs Tailwind CSS for Responsive Card Layout
This document compares Normal CSS and Tailwind CSS when building responsive card layouts. It highlights the differences between the two approaches and shows how each can be used effectively.
1. Normal CSS for Responsive Cards
CSS Code:

/* Container to hold cards */
.card-container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr; /* Default: 1 column for small screens */
  gap: 20px;
}

/* Individual card styles */
.card {
  background-color: white;
  padding: 20px;
  border-radius: 12px;
  box-shadow: 0 4px 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
}

.card h2 {
  font-size: 20px;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: center;
}

.card p {
  text-align: center;
  color: gray;
}

.card button {
  width: 100%;
  padding: 10px;
  background-color: blue;
  color: white;
  border-radius: 8px;
  border: none;
  cursor: pointer;
}

.card button:hover {
  background-color: darkblue;
}

/* Media queries for responsiveness */
@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .card-container {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); /* 2 columns for tablets */
  }
}

@media (min-width: 1024px) {
  .card-container {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); /* 3 columns for laptops */
  }
}

@media (min-width: 1280px) {
  .card-container {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); /* 4 columns for desktops */
  }
}

